# FEATURE-DOCTOR-TRUSTED-ANDROID-DEVICE-LOCK-1 Phase 2 — durable rules

Phase 2 builds the server-side control plane a future Android Clinic App will
enrol into. It is **capability only**. Phase 1
(`.cursor/rules/138-doctor-room-scoped-access-and-print-deny.mdc`) stays fully
in force and must not be weakened.

## 1. Enforcement is OFF — and structurally so

> **NARROWED by REVISION-DOCTOR-AUTO-DEVICE-APPROVAL-APP-ONLY-LOGIN-1.**
> See `.cursor/rules/143-doctor-device-authorization-and-app-only-login.mdc`
> and ADR 0011. The "nothing may read `DoctorDevice`" clause below is
> superseded; **everything else in this rule still stands**.

Enforcement is still OFF: `doctor.trusted_device_enforcement` defaults to false,
a doctor logs in exactly as before, and an **empty registry locks nobody out**.

What changed is only *how* that is asserted. The app-only gate cannot exist while
the login path is forbidden from asking whether a doctor's session may exist, so
the auth surfaces may now reference exactly `DoctorAppLoginGate`,
`DoctorDeviceSessionService` and `EnsureDoctorDeviceSession` — and nothing else.
A proof service, a direct authorization query or a second read of the enforcement
flag in an auth surface is still a failure.

`DoctorDeviceAccessTest` asserts that narrowed rule. If you are about to add a
reference outside that allow-list, you are starting Phase 4, not fixing a test —
get the enforcement decision made first.

Do not add a feature flag "in preparation". The minimum-scope decision was
deliberate: no flag exists until the phase that actually needs one.

## 2. One authority

`App\Modules\DoctorDevice\Services\DoctorDeviceService` is the ONLY place that
writes a device row. Controllers stay thin; the repository is a data gateway.

## 3. Status is never a payload

`status`, `identity_state`, `public_key_fingerprint`, `uuid` and every
`registered_*` / `disabled_*` / `revoked_*` stamp are **outside** the model's
`$fillable`, and the service writes only explicit allow-listed arrays. A
request can never drive a lifecycle transition, however it is crafted.

`updateMetadata()` edits safe metadata only. If you add a column, decide
deliberately whether it belongs in `EDITABLE_METADATA` — the default is no.

## 4. REVOKED is terminal

```
ACTIVE ⇄ DISABLED        ACTIVE → REVOKED        DISABLED → REVOKED
REVOKED → (nothing)
```

`reactivate()` refuses a revoked device. Reusing the physical hardware means a
**fresh cryptographic identity** in a later phase, never resurrecting the old
row. Disable and revoke both require a written reason; revoke is deliberately
high-friction in the UI.

## 5. Never delete a device

There is no `destroy` route, no soft delete, no service method. A device that
was ever trusted keeps its security history. Withdrawal of trust is REVOKED.

## 6. A typed row is not a proven device

`identity_state` is orthogonal to `status`:

- `unverified` — an administrator typed this in. A database record, nothing more.
- `cryptographically_verified` — the device answered a server challenge with its
  hardware-backed key. **Reserved for Phase 3 and unreachable in Phase 2.**

Never let an admin path set the verified state. Misleading trust state is worse
than no trust state.

## 7. Branch binding is server-authoritative

A device belongs to one branch, which must be an active RME-enabled branch
(`BranchService::rmeEnabledIds()`). A request may only SELECT from that set;
`branch_id` is re-validated in the service on create AND on update. Canonical
branch codes only — `TLK1`, `SPN4`; never the historical `TKM1`/`SUN4` aliases.

## 8. No MAC address, no IMEI

Neither is an authentication authority and neither is stored. The future trust
anchor is `public_key_fingerprint` — a public key fingerprint, never a private
key and never a reusable enrolment secret. Any future enrolment token must be
short-lived, single-use, rate-limited and stored hashed.

## 9. Authorization is Super Admin only

`view_doctor_devices` / `manage_doctor_devices` are seeded but granted to **no
role**, so only Super Admin reaches them via the single global `Gate::before` —
the ENT-7 `view_developer_console` precedent. Do not grant them to Doctor,
Kasir, Admin Klinik, Perawat or Owner without an explicit governance decision.

Read and write are separate abilities: `view_doctor_devices` alone passes the
route middleware, so the **policy** is what refuses every write.

The sidebar is never the boundary.

## 10. Audit every transition

`DOCTOR_DEVICE_CREATED` / `_UPDATED` / `_DISABLED` / `_REACTIVATED` / `_REVOKED`
through the shared `AuditLogService` (`sys_audit_logs`). Payload is
identifiers, status and the operator's reason only — never the key fingerprint,
never a secret, never patient data.

## 11. Out of scope for Phase 2

No Android app, no enrolment API, no challenge/response endpoint, no session
binding, no device-aware login. A clean domain boundary beats speculative API
breadth; build the endpoint in the phase that has a client for it.
